# 🔥 Vector of Pairs — C++ vs Java vs Python

This is especially important because you've just learned `pair`.

Suppose we want:

```text
1 → One
2 → Two
3 → Three
```

### C++

```cpp
vector<pair<int, string>> v;

v.push_back({1, "One"});
v.push_back({2, "Two"});
v.emplace_back(3, "Three");

cout << v[0].first << " " << v[0].second;
```

Access:

```cpp
v[0].first
v[0].second
```

---

### Java

Using `SimpleEntry`:

```java
import java.util.*;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        ArrayList<AbstractMap.SimpleEntry<Integer, String>> list =
            new ArrayList<>();

        list.add(new AbstractMap.SimpleEntry<>(1, "One"));
        list.add(new AbstractMap.SimpleEntry<>(2, "Two"));
        list.add(new AbstractMap.SimpleEntry<>(3, "Three"));

        System.out.println(
            list.get(0).getKey() + " " +
            list.get(0).getValue()
        );
    }
}
```

Access:

```text
get(0)       → get the pair from ArrayList
getKey()     → first value
getValue()   → second value
```

So:

```java
list.get(0).getKey()
list.get(0).getValue()
```

---

### Python

Python makes this very simple:

```python
v = []

v.append((1, "One"))
v.append((2, "Two"))
v.append((3, "Three"))

print(v[0][0], v[0][1])
```

Output:

```text
1 One
```

You can also write:

```python
for x, y in v:
    print(x, y)
```

Output:

```text
1 One
2 Two
3 Three
```

